II. The American Rupture: PJM and the First Fracture

The most visible fracture line runs through the territory of PJM Interconnection, the grid operator responsible for 65 million Americans across 13 states from Chicago to the Atlantic seaboard. PJM's footprint contains the densest concentration of data center capacity in North America—the server farms of Northern Virginia that power a substantial fraction of the world's cloud computing and AI inference.

On December 17, 2025, PJM cleared its 2027/28 capacity auction at $333.44 per megawatt-day, the administratively imposed price ceiling, across every zone of its footprint. The auction fell 6,623 megawatts short of the reliability requirement—the first time in the operator's history that the entire regional transmission organization missed its own reliability target. The reserve margin printed 14.8 percent against a 20 percent target, the lowest in PJM's history. Of the 5,250 megawatts of year-over-year load growth, 5,100 megawatts were explicitly attributed by PJM to data center demand.

The price signal was even starker. Absent the price cap, the shadow clearing price would have been $529.80 per megawatt-day. In the Dominion zone—the epicenter of North American data center capacity—the uncapped price would have reached $542.83.These are not utility prices. They are scarcity prices, the kind of market signal that typically precedes physical rationing.

The gap between procurement and requirement is widening, not narrowing. In the 2026/27 auction, the shortfall was approximately 210 megawatts. In the 2027/28 auction, it rose to 6,520 megawatts—a thirtyfold increase in a single year.

The financial consequences are already materializing. Wholesale power costs in PJM jumped 54% in one year, from $43.5 billion in 2024 to $67 billion in 2025. Capacity costs—the payments for the mere availability of generation—increased 262% over the same period.Monitoring Analytics, PJM's independent market monitor, estimated that the inclusion of data center load growth in the last three capacity auctions resulted in $23.1 billion in additional system costs.

In response, the Trump administration and all 13 PJM governors jointly called for an emergency capacity auction in February 2026. PJM itself proposed a fast-track 15-gigawatt initiative that would bypass normal market processes, directly pairing data center projects with new generation through bilateral negotiation.The fact that a grid operator is asking for 15 gigawatts—the equivalent of more than a dozen large nuclear plants—on an emergency timeline is itself an admission that normal processes have failed.

But the emergency response collides with the same physical constraints that created the emergency. The Crane Clean Energy Center—the restart of the Three Mile Island Unit 1 reactor that Microsoft is backing with an 835-megawatt power purchase agreement—may not fully connect to the PJM grid until 2031, four years later than the original 2027 target, due to transmission project delays.Even when generation exists, the wires to deliver it do not.

