IV. The Texas Paradox

Texas presents a paradox that illuminates the broader crisis.

No state has moved more aggressively to attract AI infrastructure. Low electricity prices, a streamlined regulatory environment, and abundant land have drawn more than 230 gigawatts of proposed data center capacity into the ERCOT interconnection queue. Data centers account for 77% of the large-load interconnection requests in Texas.

ERCOT projects that electricity demand will more than quadruple to approximately 368 gigawatts by 2032, with more than 60% of that growth coming from data centers alone.

But the queue is not the grid. ERCOT's peak supply capacity is approximately 85 gigawatts—already falling short of even the existing interconnection queue, let alone projected demand. The queue for large-load interconnection requests has swollen to more than 233 gigawatts, a 269% increase from 2025.More than 2,000 generation interconnection requests are pending, totaling over 453,000 megawatts—nearly five times the grid's current peak capacity.

The Texas solution to this congestion is revealing. Senate Bill 6 and companion regulatory reforms introduce a batch processing system for interconnection applications. Projects in "Batch Zero"—those that can demonstrate near-term readiness—will receive priority grid access. Projects not selected for Batch Zero may wait years for the necessary transmission and generation to be built.

The batch system is rational triage. It acknowledges that the grid cannot serve all comers and that some projects must be prioritized over others. But it also creates a winner-take-all dynamic. Developers who secure Batch Zero slots will capture the available capacity. Everyone else will wait indefinitely. The scramble for Batch Zero is, in effect, a scramble for the right to exist.

ERCOT itself has expressed concern about its own forecasting. In a filing to the Public Utility Commission, the grid operator explicitly warned that it has "concerns with using the preliminary load forecast values" for reliability assessment and transmission planning. The numbers are too large, the timeline too compressed, the underlying assumptions too uncertain.

The paradox is this: Texas has more proposed AI infrastructure than any other state, but it also has the most visible congestion of any American grid. The very factors that attract data centers—low prices, fast permitting—are creating a queue that will strand most of them.

V. Europe: The Quiet Crisis

The European grid crisis is less dramatic but no less consequential.

European data center IT power capacity grew from 10,539 megawatts in 2023 to 14,784 megawatts in 2025, exceeding forecasts. Cumulative investment from 2026 to 2031 is expected to reach €176 billion. Yet the European Data Centre Association warns that "future capacity growth will be limited primarily by grid readiness rather than capital availability."

Grid connection has become the defining constraint for European data center projects. In Germany, only 10% to 15% of grid applications for data centers are expected to be accepted. The "first come, first served" principle that once governed European grid access has broken down under the weight of excess reservation requests. Projects that fail to secure realistic and timely grid connections risk becoming stranded long before construction begins.

The pattern of constraint varies by region, but the underlying dynamic is consistent. In Switzerland, there are fears that Zurich's grid no longer has capacity for additional demand. In Italy, grid congestion, long connection queues, and limited substation capacity increasingly determine where new facilities can be developed. Across the European Union, 67% of data center operators cite power availability as their greatest operational challenge.

The European response has been to accelerate the shift toward secondary markets. Nordic countries, with their abundant hydropower and cooler climates, are expected to grow at double the rate of core European markets over the next several years. Spain, Portugal, and Italy are attracting increasing volumes of capital as developers seek locations where grid capacity still exists.

But this relocation is not a solution to the grid constraint; it is a migration away from it. The electrons cannot be moved, so the data centers move to the electrons. The map of European AI infrastructure is being redrawn not by strategic choice but by substation availability.

VI. China: The Contained Crisis

China's grid constraint is qualitatively different from those in the United States and Europe—not because China has solved the problem, but because it started from a different baseline.

As documented in Chapter 2, China maintains grid reserve margins of 80% to 100% nationwide. Substations are built in 48 weeks, not 143. Electricity prices for industrial users are less than half those in the United States and a third of those in Europe. China added more power capacity across every energy technology since 2021 than the United States has built in its entire history.

But China is not immune to the reckoning. Its challenge is different: not whether it can build the infrastructure, but whether it can build it fast enough to keep pace with demand that is accelerating even faster.

China's electricity consumption reached 10,368 terawatt-hours in 2025, and the China Electricity Council projects 2026 consumption will reach 10.9 to 11.0 trillion kilowatt-hours, growing 5% to 6%. Peak load is expected to reach 1.57 to 1.63 billion kilowatts, a new record.

The demand driver is increasingly AI and data centers. Data center electricity consumption in China reached 120 billion kilowatt-hours in 2025, approximately 1.1% of national consumption. By 2030, that figure could grow to 390 to 820 billion kilowatt-hours, representing 3% to 5.3% of total consumption.

China is responding with characteristic scale. In 2026 alone, China is expected to add more than 400 gigawatts of new generation capacity, including over 300 gigawatts of renewable energy. Total installed capacity will reach approximately 4,300 gigawatts by year-end, with non-fossil sources accounting for roughly 63%.

The difference between China's situation and that of the United States is not the existence of a constraint but its nature. The U.S. constraint is a supply shortage: there is not enough generation capacity, not enough transmission, not enough transformers. The Chinese constraint is a coordination challenge: aligning the explosive growth of AI infrastructure with the already-rapid expansion of the grid.

Neither is trivial. But one is solvable with continued investment; the other is a structural deficit that will take years to correct even with unlimited capital.

