Chapter 7: The Sovereign Solution — How Nationalizing the Grid Becomes a Matter of AI Security

The Question No One Wanted to Ask

For four decades, the proposition that the federal government should assert greater control over the electrical grid has been politically unmentionable. The restructuring of the 1990s and 2000s—the great experiment in electricity deregulation—was premised on the belief that markets, not central planners, would deliver the most efficient outcomes. Regional transmission organizations (RTOs) and independent system operators (ISOs) would manage competitive wholesale markets. Private capital would fund generation and transmission. The invisible hand would keep the lights on.

The AI revolution has exposed the limits of that premise.

The grid constraints documented in the preceding chapters—five-year interconnection queues, 144-week transformer lead times, capacity auctions clearing at scarcity prices—are not temporary market inefficiencies. They are structural features of a system designed for a different era. The American electricity grid was optimized for slow, predictable demand growth and minimal new infrastructure. It was not optimized for a world in which a single AI data center campus can demand more power than a mid-sized city, and in which the nation's economic and military competitiveness depends on how quickly that power can be delivered.

The question that was once unmentionable is now being asked openly in Washington, Beijing, London, and Brussels: in the age of AI, can the market deliver the grid the nation needs, or must the state step in?

This chapter examines the emerging answers. They are not uniform. The United States is pursuing a hybrid approach—federal preemption of interconnection queues without full nationalization. China is doubling down on its state-owned, centrally planned model. The United Kingdom is pioneering a new financing mechanism that blurs the line between public and private infrastructure. Europe is caught between its climate ambitions and the hard reality of permitting paralysis.

The common thread is the recognition that electricity is no longer a commodity to be traded. It is a strategic asset to be secured. The sovereign solution is not a single policy. It is a spectrum of interventions, each reflecting a different judgment about how much control the state must exercise over the electrons that power the AI era.

---

The American Hybrid: Federal Preemption Without Nationalization

The United States is not nationalizing its grid. The political coalition required for such a move does not exist, and the institutional capacity to execute it does not reside in any federal agency. But the United States is doing something that, in the context of its federalist tradition, is nearly as significant: it is asserting federal jurisdiction over the process that has become the primary bottleneck to AI infrastructure.

The mechanism is a legal doctrine that has lain dormant for decades. On October 23, 2025, Energy Secretary Chris Wright invoked special authority under the Department of Energy Organization Act to send a formal directive to the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC). The directive ordered FERC to develop and issue a Final Rule standardizing and accelerating the interconnection process for "large loads" of 20 megawatts or more—a category that includes virtually all AI data centers.

The legal basis for the directive is as significant as its content. Secretary Wright asserted that "the interconnection of large loads directly to the interstate transmission system to access the transmission system and the electricity transmitted over it falls squarely within the Commission's jurisdiction" under the Federal Power Act, even though FERC has not historically exerted jurisdiction over load interconnections. The Department of Energy argued that large load interconnections are a "critical component of open access transmission service" that require minimum terms and conditions to ensure non-discriminatory service, and that such interconnections directly affect wholesale electricity rates, which FERC is mandated to keep just, reasonable, and non-discriminatory.

The practical implications are substantial. The proposed reforms seek to reduce study times for grid interconnection requests, lower barriers for co-location of load and generation, ensure large loads pay the cost of network upgrades required for them to interconnect reliably, and introduce readiness requirements and penalties to deter speculative projects. The DOE set an aggressive deadline for FERC to issue a Final Rule by April 30, 2026.

The directive is not nationalization. It is preemption. The federal government is not taking ownership of the grid; it is asserting control over the process by which new users gain access to it. But the effect is similar: a federal agency, not a patchwork of state regulators and regional transmission organizations, will set the rules that determine how quickly AI infrastructure can connect to the power it requires.

FERC has already begun to act on this new mandate. On December 18, 2025, the Commission issued a unanimous order directing PJM Interconnection—the grid operator for 67 million Americans across 13 states—to establish transparent rules for "co-locating" data centers at power plants. The order effectively allows AI facilities to plug directly into nuclear and gas-fired generators, bypassing traditional grid interconnection queues that currently stretch five to seven years in many regions.

The co-location ruling represents a fundamental shift in the architecture of the grid. Data centers are no longer merely customers taking service from the transmission system. They are becoming partners with generators, co-located at the point of production, drawing power before it ever enters the bulk transmission network. Commissioner Rosner articulated the logic plainly: "If a new large load wants to connect directly to a power plant and operate in a way that lowers grid costs, we should let it. If the current rules don't let this work in a way that's fair for everyone, we must change those rules."

The legislative branch has joined the effort. The REWIRE Act, sponsored by Senators Dave McCormick (R-Pa.) and Peter Welch (D-Vt.), would ease environmental permitting requirements for projects that increase grid capacity by upgrading existing transmission lines with advanced conductors. The CERTAIN Act aims to transform the federal permitting process more broadly, streamlining environmental review and accelerating decision-making.

Perhaps most radical is the DATA Act of 2026, introduced by Senator Tom Cotton (R-Ark.). The bill creates a new category of electric utility called a "consumer-regulated electric utility," or CREU, which would be exempt from federal rate regulation, reliability standards, and interconnection requirements—provided the CREU is physically islanded from the bulk power system and serves only new loads. The DATA Act essentially creates a parallel electricity system for AI data centers, one that operates outside the regulatory framework that governs the public grid.

The bill is controversial. Utilities oppose it because it threatens their revenue base. Environmental groups worry about the proliferation of behind-the-meter fossil fuel generation. Reliability advocates question whether an unregulated, islanded system can maintain the same standards as the interconnected grid. But the very fact that such a proposal is under serious consideration in the United States Senate indicates how far the Overton window has shifted. Five years ago, the idea of exempting large loads from federal utility regulation would have been dismissed as a libertarian fantasy. Today, it is one of several legislative vehicles attempting to solve the grid interconnection crisis.

The American approach is not a single, coherent policy. It is a set of overlapping interventions—federal preemption of interconnection queues, co-location mandates, permitting reform, and exemptions for off-grid generation—that collectively amount to an assertion of federal authority over a domain that was once the exclusive province of states and markets. The grid remains privately owned. But the rules governing access to it are increasingly being written in Washington.

