China: The State-Owned Model Perfected

If the United States is reluctantly edging toward federal preemption, China is executing the full state-owned model with a coherence and speed that Western policymakers can only observe with unease.

The State Grid Corporation of China is the world's largest utility, responsible for transmission and distribution across most of the country. It is a wholly state-owned enterprise, directed by the central government's strategic priorities and funded by a combination of state capital, policy bank lending, and retained earnings. Its planning horizon is measured in decades, not quarters. Its mandate is national development, not shareholder returns.

The difference in outcomes is stark. As documented in Chapter 2, China builds substations in 48 weeks, not 143. It maintains grid reserve margins of 80% to 100%, not 15%. It adds more than 400 gigawatts of new generation capacity annually, roughly six times the pace of the United States.

But the quantitative differences are less revealing than the qualitative ones. China is not merely building more infrastructure; it is building a different kind of infrastructure. In September 2025, the National Development and Reform Commission and the National Energy Administration jointly released a plan to accelerate the integration of artificial intelligence into the energy sector. The plan sets a goal of establishing a basic innovation system for AI-energy integration by 2027 and achieving globally leading AI applications in the energy field by 2030.

The plan is not aspirational. It is operational. It calls for the application of over five industry-specific AI models in state grids, electricity generation, and oil and gas; the launch of ten replicable demonstration projects; and the exploration of one hundred typical application scenarios. The State Grid Corporation has already developed the "Guangming" foundation model—a large AI model designed specifically for power system operations—which won the SAIL Award at the 2025 World Artificial Intelligence Conference.

In December 2025, the NDRC and NEA issued a further guidance document on high-quality grid development. It set specific, quantifiable targets: by 2030, the "West-to-East" power transmission capacity will exceed 420 gigawatts, new inter-provincial power exchange capacity will reach 40 gigawatts, renewable energy will account for approximately 30% of generation, and distributed energy resource hosting capacity will reach 900 gigawatts. By 2035, the grid will be fully integrated across transmission, distribution, and microgrid layers, with intelligent, digitalized management across the entire asset lifecycle.

The document's language is revealing. It refers to the "People's Electricity for the People" as the core principle, affirming the grid's role as "public infrastructure" and its duty to serve national development objectives. There is no distinction between economic efficiency and national strategy. The two are identical.

China's approach to AI infrastructure is therefore not a separate policy from its grid policy. It is the same policy. The country that can build transmission lines faster, connect data centers more quickly, and power AI workloads more reliably will win the AI race. China has made winning that race an explicit state priority, leveraging hundreds of billions in state-directed investment and building grid infrastructure at a scale unimaginable in the United States today.

The implications for robotics are direct. As established in Chapter 3, the robot energy budget is the terminal node of a system whose root is the electrical substation. China's ability to deliver abundant, reliable, and affordable power to AI data centers translates directly into faster training runs, more capable models, and more deployable robots. The state-owned grid is not merely an input to China's AI strategy. It is the foundation on which the entire strategy rests.

---

The British Innovation: The Regulated Asset Base

The United Kingdom occupies an intermediate position on the spectrum of sovereign intervention. It has not nationalized its grid, but it has pioneered a financing mechanism that fundamentally alters the relationship between private capital and public infrastructure.

The mechanism is the Regulated Asset Base (RAB) model. Under traditional infrastructure financing, developers must raise capital up front, bear construction risk, and begin receiving revenue only when the asset is operational. For nuclear power plants, which cost tens of billions of dollars and take a decade or more to build, this model has proven unworkable. Projects have been canceled, investors have fled, and the pipeline of new nuclear capacity has stalled across the Western world.

The RAB model changes the calculus. Under RAB, a project receives a regulated payment from electricity suppliers during the construction phase, not merely after commissioning. The payments provide a return on capital from the moment of investment, enabling debt servicing and equity returns even as construction proceeds. The model lowers the cost of financing by reducing the risk premium that investors demand for bearing construction risk. The government estimates that overall consumers will save more than £30 billion over the lifetime of each new large-scale nuclear power station compared with existing funding mechanisms.

The RAB model is being deployed first at Sizewell C, a 3.2-gigawatt nuclear plant in Suffolk. The project's Final Investment Decision was approved in July 2025, with the UK government taking a 44.9% equity stake and becoming the largest shareholder. The estimated capital cost is approximately $51 billion in 2024 prices. British Centrica acquired a 15% stake for £1.3 billion, attracted by the inflation-protected returns and the 10.8% return on equity during construction.

The RAB model is not nationalization. The assets remain privately owned, and private capital provides the majority of funding. But the model does represent a significant expansion of the state's role. The government is effectively underwriting the financing costs, absorbing the risk that private capital is unwilling to bear, and guaranteeing a return to investors during the construction period. In exchange, the project proceeds, the grid gains baseload capacity, and the nation secures a strategic asset.

The model is being extended to small modular reactors. Great British Energy—Nuclear is expected to identify additional sites for future large-scale and SMR projects in 2026. Rolls-Royce SMR has received a commitment of up to £599 million from the National Wealth Fund to advance its designs. The UK is betting that the RAB model can unlock the capital required for a nuclear renaissance, without requiring the full state ownership that prevails in China.

The RAB model is a sovereign solution in the sense that it acknowledges a market failure—private capital's unwillingness to finance long-dated, capital-intensive infrastructure—and addresses it with a state-backed financing mechanism. It is not socialism. It is recognition that certain assets are too important, and too difficult to finance through conventional markets, to be left entirely to the private sector. The state does not own the asset. But the state makes the asset possible.

---

The Nuclear Licensing Revolution

The sovereign solution extends beyond grid access and financing. It also encompasses the regulatory framework governing the generation assets that will power the AI era—particularly nuclear reactors.

The Nuclear Regulatory Commission has historically been one of the slowest-moving agencies in the federal government. The primary framework for licensing nuclear reactors was established in 1956 and designed for large-scale light-water reactors. For decades, innovators seeking to build advanced reactors—cooled by molten salt, liquid metal, or high-temperature gas—had to spend millions of dollars and thousands of man-hours requesting exemptions from rules that did not apply to their technology.

That changed in March 2026. The NRC finalized 10 CFR Part 53, the most significant regulatory shift in nuclear power in seventy years. The new framework is technology-inclusive and risk-informed. Instead of prescribing how a reactor must be built, it asks whether the design can demonstrate that it poses minimal risk to the public under any credible scenario.

The practical impact is substantial. The NRC has cut its hourly fee for licensing reviews from approximately $318 to $148, lowering licensing costs that can run into tens of millions of dollars for complex designs. An Executive Order issued by President Trump in May 2025 directed the NRC to aim for approximately 18-month final decisions on new reactor applications. The Department of Energy has selected 11 small reactor projects for a pilot program targeting at least three new plants operating by July 4, 2026.

The reform is not merely administrative. It is strategic. The NRC finalized Part 53 nearly two years ahead of the deadline mandated by the Nuclear Energy Innovation and Modernization Act—a rare instance of a federal agency beating a legislative deadline, underscoring the urgency felt within the Department of Energy and the White House to secure American leadership in nuclear exports.

The nuclear licensing revolution is a sovereign solution in the sense that it removes a state-imposed barrier to infrastructure deployment. The bottleneck was not the technology, the capital, or the demand. It was the regulatory process. By reforming that process, the state is not building reactors itself, but it is clearing the path for private capital to build them.

The parallel with grid interconnection reform is exact. In both cases, the state is not nationalizing the asset. It is reforming the process by which private actors gain access to the asset. The effect, however, is the same: faster deployment of the infrastructure required to power the AI era.

---

Europe: The Paralysis of Deliberation

Europe occupies the opposite end of the spectrum. It possesses the industrial depth, capital, research capacity, and single market to compete in the AI era. It lacks the institutional capacity to act quickly.

The European Council on Foreign Relations has warned that "slow decision-making in Europe undermines the continent's security, prosperity and political stability." The AI-driven surge in electricity demand is reshaping geopolitics, favoring states such as America and China that can rapidly expand power generation and grids. Europe risks becoming "an energy-constrained AI follower."

The symptoms are visible across the continent. In Germany, only 10% to 15% of grid applications for data centers are expected to be accepted. Spain has had to cap new energy production because it lacks sufficient grid capacity. Mario Draghi, the former European Central Bank president, noted that Europe is in danger of losing up to ten times more renewable energy generation than it loses today because of grid constraint capacity.

The ECFR's prescription is a "fast energy" program to speed up permitting, grid buildout, and deployment of clean power. But the institutional barriers to such a program are formidable. European decision-making is distributed across 27 member states, each with its own regulatory framework, permitting processes, and political constituencies. The European Union can set targets and provide funding, but it cannot compel member states to approve transmission lines or issue construction permits.

The contrast with China is instructive. China's "West-to-East" power transmission policy is a single national directive executed by a single state-owned enterprise. The State Grid Corporation builds the lines, connects the generation, and manages the flow. There is no equivalent institution in Europe. The European Network of Transmission System Operators for Electricity (ENTSO-E) coordinates among national grid operators but lacks the authority to compel investment or override local opposition.

Europe's paralysis is not a failure of policy. It is a feature of its political architecture. The same deliberative processes that protect member state sovereignty and ensure democratic accountability also prevent the rapid infrastructure deployment that the AI era demands. The sovereign solution, in Europe's case, may be the recognition that the existing institutional framework is inadequate—and that a new, more centralized approach is required.

---

The Geopolitical Imperative

The sovereign solutions described in this chapter—federal preemption in the United States, state ownership in China, RAB financing in the United Kingdom, regulatory reform in the nuclear sector—share a common premise. Electricity is no longer a commodity. It is a strategic asset, as essential to national security in the twenty-first century as oil was in the twentieth.

The geopolitical implications are becoming explicit. "We believe fundamentally that energy supply is a national security imperative. Winning the AI race requires winning the energy race," stated one senior official, noting that rivals operate under fewer constraints. The European Council on Foreign Relations has observed that "a global AI-driven surge in electricity demand is reshaping geopolitics, favouring states such as America and China that can rapidly expand power generation and grids."

The Searchlight Institute, a technology policy think tank, has concluded that "accelerating competition between the U.S. and China for dominance in artificial intelligence, advanced semiconductors, and large-scale computation has made data center infrastructure a national security asset: the country that can site, power, and connect facilities fastest has a decisive advantage in a race that will define the next generation of economic and military power."

The implications for robotics are direct. As established in Chapter 5, robots will be built where the power is. The Pearl River Delta, the American energy belt, and the Arabian Peninsula are positioned to capture a disproportionate share of robotics manufacturing and deployment. But the sovereign solution adds a new dimension: the state that can most effectively mobilize infrastructure investment will determine not only where robots are built, but how quickly they improve.

The Grid-to-Gearbox Chain, introduced in Chapter 3, applies with equal force to the sovereign solution. The training of robot foundation models requires data centers. Data centers require power. Power requires generation, transmission, and distribution infrastructure. The state that can accelerate infrastructure deployment—through federal preemption, state ownership, innovative financing, or regulatory reform—accelerates the entire chain.

The sovereign solution is not a single policy. It is a recognition that the market, left to its own devices, cannot deliver the infrastructure required for the AI era at the speed the competition demands. The state must intervene. The only question is how.

---

Conclusion

The AI race has inverted the relationship between the state and the grid.

For decades, the prevailing wisdom held that markets allocate capital more efficiently than governments, that private ownership produces better outcomes than public ownership, and that the state's role in the electricity sector should be limited to regulation and oversight. The restructuring of the 1990s and 2000s was the institutional expression of that wisdom.

The AI revolution has exposed the limits of that model. The infrastructure required to power the AI era—gigawatt-scale data centers, high-voltage transmission lines, advanced nuclear reactors—is too large, too long-dated, and too strategically significant to be left entirely to the market. The state must play a role. The only question is what kind of role.

The United States is pursuing federal preemption without nationalization. China is executing the full state-owned model. The United Kingdom is pioneering the RAB financing mechanism. Europe is struggling to overcome its institutional paralysis. Each approach reflects a different judgment about how much control the state must exercise over the electrons that power the AI era.

But the common thread is unmistakable. Electricity is no longer a commodity to be traded on competitive markets. It is a strategic asset to be secured by the state. The sovereign solution is not a return to the command-and-control utilities of the mid-twentieth century. It is a new form of state intervention, adapted to the demands of the AI era, and justified by the national security imperative of winning the AI race.

The firms that survive the reckoning will be those that understand this shift. They will secure their "Electron Rights" not through market transactions alone, but through partnerships with the state—federal preemption of interconnection queues, co-location agreements with regulated generators, access to RAB-financed nuclear capacity. The era of pure market-driven infrastructure deployment is over. The era of the sovereign solution has begun.

The AI race is not decided in a server room. It is decided at the power source, and the power source is increasingly a matter of state.


Chapter 8: The Autonomy Century — What a World Powered by Robots Actually Looks Like

